{"availability":"available","full":"JonOnTheHub/ai-ops-assistant","name":"ai-ops-assistant","description":"A constrained, auditable AI operations agent that earns permission before acting. Most AI agents freestyle with full tool access. Warrant does the opposite: every tool is gated by permission tiers (auto / log-and-run / needs-approval), every action is fully traced, and high-impact actions (like sending emails) require human approval.","category":"Developer tools","language":"TypeScript","stars":1,"forks":0,"created":"2026-07-07T11:17:54Z","updated":"2026-10-08T23:30:15Z","branch":"main","homepage":"https://usewarrant.vercel.app/","topics":[],"demo":"https://usewarrant.vercel.app/","color":"#25392f","ink":"#d1e5c5","lastCheckedAt":"2026-10-08T23:42:54.638Z","lastAttemptAt":"2026-10-08T23:42:54.638Z","lastAvailableAt":"2026-10-08T23:42:54.638Z","metrics":[{"at":"2026-10-08T23:42:54.638Z","stars":1,"forks":0}],"technologies":[],"overview":{"kind":"readme","version":1,"paragraphs":["An AI operations assistant that doesn't act without earning the right to.","Most \"AI agent\" demos let the model freestyle — call any tool, chain any action, and execute without guardrails. Warrant is the opposite bet: a constrained, deterministic agent where every tool sits behind a permission tier, every decision leaves a trace, and anything that leaves the system (like sending an email to a real customer) waits for human approval — and that approval reports back honestly, not optimistically.","A warrant is permission that has to be earned before an action proceeds. That is exactly how Warrant behaves. The model never decides what it is allowed to do — it proposes, the permission layer decides, and the system reports back honestly about what actually happened."],"features":["🔎 RAG knowledge search","✅ Task & lead creation","✉️ Email drafting & sending","📜 Execution tracing","🖥️ Live trace viewer"],"sourceUrl":"https://github.com/JonOnTheHub/ai-ops-assistant/blob/main/README.md","fing